{
 "_": "THE MATERIAL REGISTRY. 48 semantic slots, one definition each, and one block of values per treatment. Nothing in the model may carry a colour that is not defined here.",
 "_why": "Before this file the model carried 164 materials wearing 34 names, and the same name meant different things in different places: wall_brick had EIGHT different definitions, door seven, roof_slate six. Four modelling libraries had each chosen their own values. That is why a change to 'the brick' had to be made eight times and still came out inconsistent, and it is the reason a treatment could not be switched at all - there was nothing to switch. Semantic tokens are the precondition for options, not a consequence of them.",
 "_axes": {
  "treatment": "What the model is made of. drawing | estate | estate-rake | estate-wash | estate-wear | fabric",
  "light": "How it is lit. overcast | raking | bright",
  "_note": "The two are orthogonal on purpose: a treatment never encodes light, a light never encodes material. Three of each compose into nine looks for six units of work. Selected combination is URL-addressable in the viewer as ?variants=1&treatment=estate&light=raking"
 },
 "_directions": {
  "drawing": "White card. The architectural model register - material read by tone and shadow alone, near-monochrome, the light doing all the work. What a museum model of the estate would look like.",
  "estate": "Painted. The real materials as considered flat colour: Berkshire red brick, Welsh slate, Portland ashlar, lead. No texture, but every value chosen against the real thing.",
  "fabric": "Built. Full physically-based materials from the 20-set texture library, projected triplanar so it lands on every mesh including the LIDAR reconstructions and the terrain, which have no UVs.",
  "estate-rake": "Estate gravel option. Rake: corrected raking-light gravel with restrained metre-scale rake variation.",
  "estate-wash": "Estate gravel option. Wash: corrected gravel with restrained baked wash variation.",
  "estate-wear": "Estate gravel option. Wear: corrected gravel with restrained wash plus inferred vehicle wear.",
  "overcast": "Soft high cloud. Even light, gentle shadow, cool. Shows plan and layout; flatters nothing.",
  "raking": "Low sun, late in the day. Long shadows, warm key against a cool sky fill. Shows landform, roof pitch and depth of modelling - the light a detailer judges by.",
  "bright": "High sun, clear day. Short crisp shadows, strong contrast, saturated greens. The estate at its most legible."
 },
 "_slot_note": "role says what the slot is FOR. texture names a set in textures/ for the fabric treatment; null means the slot is untextured in every treatment (vertex-coloured foliage, overlay, ghosted massing). tile_m is the physical size of one texture repeat in metres - a brick course must read at the right size or the building looks like a doll's house. RECORD: the brick TILE defect is estate-wide and is NOT fixed here. handmade-red-brick carries 40 courses and is stretched over 2.0 m, giving a 50 mm course where a real brick with its bed joint is 75 mm; make_textures.py declares that material as 4 x 3 m. Every other brick building is drawn about a third too small. Angus has ruled not to change it now.",
 "slots": {
  "wall_brick": {
   "role": "Principal walling brick - Main House, courtyards, stables",
   "texture": "handmade-red-brick",
   "tile_m": 2.0
  },
  "wall_brick_courtyard": {
   "role": "RETIRED BY RULING, 28 Aug 2026 - registered, bound by nothing, and must stay that way",
   "_retired": "This was the courtyard precinct's own weathered-stock brick, and it stood for one evening. Angus reversed it: \"I specifically requested a different texture to that of the main house but the courtyard buildings and service yard should match the main house. Same brick and matching stone and portico colour.\" The precinct draws wall_brick now, and the only references left to this slot are its own registration - this file, the two treatment files, and the note in assets/kit/courtyard_style.py. IT IS KEPT RATHER THAN DELETED because deleting a registered slot is a change the treatments and courtyard_style's own 'every material is a registry slot' check all read, and because the decision to leave it registered and unused is itself on the record in COURTYARD.md. DO NOT BIND IT: a future session that finds an unused brick slot and puts it to work would quietly reintroduce the second brick he had just removed, and nobody would catch it in a render because a weathered stock at a 3.0 m tile looks like a brick. Flagged by estate-review-6, 29 Aug 2026, on the same argument as the do-not-merge note on wall_stone and stone_detail.",
   "texture": "brick-weathered-stock",
   "tile_m": 3.0
  },
  "wall_stone": {
   "role": "Dressed stone walling",
   "_retired_sandy": "THE SANDY VALUE IS RETIRED, Angus 29 Aug 2026, and this slot now carries stone_detail's pale colour in every treatment. His reason, given 30 Aug: the courtyard rebuild used the white for its French features, that white is closer to the Main House than the sandy some of the house's own features carried, and the task was to bring the HOUSE to the courtyard's white - \"so no sandy\". Measured, and it is why the ruling bites here rather than in an asset: dressed stone by surface area is 11,600 m2 sandy against 1,473 pale on the Main House and 2,689 against 122 on its north wing, but 813 against 6,086 on the south courtyard and 3,305 against 6,427 on the north. The courtyard was already pale; the house was not. One colour changes 14,289 m2 of house and wing without touching a single asset. STANDARD.md's own primary-photograph line has described those surrounds as PALE since 19 August and no provenance exists anywhere for the sandy value. THE RULING WAS RECORDED AS DONE ON 29 AUG AND NEVER APPLIED - COURTYARD.md said \"in all four treatments\" while the number had not moved since the first commit. DO NOT MERGE THIS SLOT WITH stone_detail: they now hold the same colour and differ by tile scale, 2.4 m against 1.2 m, and collapsing them would halve the ashlar on twelve assets.",
   "texture": "ashlar-stone",
   "tile_m": 2.4
  },
