  "wallbrick": "colour is pure white because the slot carries an image texture (scratch/wall_tile.png) as its base colour. Under the drawing and estate treatments it must take a real value, not white.",
  "terrain": "same - white because the baked albedo image carries the colour. The baked image ALSO contains canopy shadow, which the renderer then casts again (STANDARD.md R20).",
  "leaf_*": "white by design: vertex colours drive the tint. Leave them white in every treatment.",
  "roof vs roof_slate": "two slots for one thing - `roof` is the reconstruction roof and is brown, `roof_slate` is the modelled roof and is near-black. They should not be different colours for the same material; resolve when the reconstructions are hand-modelled in Stage 3."
 },
 "_notes": {
  "wall_stone": "THE SANDY DRESSED STONE RETIRES, 29 Aug 2026. wall_stone takes stone_detail's value in every treatment: 0.455,0.428,0.368 -> 0.512,0.484,0.418.\nTHE EVIDENCE, which is stronger than the instruction and is why this is not a preference: STANDARD.md's primary-photograph line, the one that settles the Main House entrance elevation from 'Fawley Court 5.jpg', already reads 'sashes and glazing bars are WHITE (measured #f1e5db in sun) in PALE STONE SURROUNDS'. The record has described those surrounds as pale since 19 August. No provenance exists anywhere in the tools, the assets or the docs for 0.455,0.428,0.368 - it was never measured from anything. So this brings the model into line with evidence already on its own record rather than overriding it. (Checked independently by estate-review-6, 29 Aug.)\nAngus's ruling, on seeing the newly detailed courtyard beside the house: 'these french features are also white. So its the main house that now needs to match the courtyard buildings french features... The sandy colour as far as im aware can go.'\nTWELVE ASSETS LIGHTEN, all of them checked as wanting it: main-house, main-house-north-wing, residence, courts, follies-east-park, meadows-glasshouses, remenham-riverside, remenham-village, a4155-frontage, proposed-types and two courtyard-set assets. One value per treatment to revert.\nDO NOT MERGE THESE TWO SLOTS. wall_stone and stone_detail now carry IDENTICAL COLOURS in all four treatments and they are still genuinely different slots: the roles differ - dressed stone walling against cornices, quoins, copings, plinths and balustrade - and so does tile_m, 2.4 m against 1.2 m. A future tidy-up that collapsed 'two slots with the same value' into one would silently HALVE THE ASHLAR SCALE on twelve assets. The values are equal by ruling; the slots differ by tile scale."
 }
